  <h1>About</h1>

  <p>This web-log comes out of a few different motivations. My time at the University of St Andrews, where I graduated just over a year ago, let me engage with
  economics and finance in two ways at once. Some of it was structured, through my studies, and some of it was independent, following whatever topics happened to
  catch my interest. While applying the ideas I learned in university to real world problems has been rewarding, it has tended towards the former due to its more
  limited scope. This project is my way of keeping that second strand going and, in doing so, sustaining my interest in these topics and continue to grow in my
  career.</p>

  <p>A second, more immediate motivation has been the effect of AI on my capacity to think independently and originally. As these tools have grown more capable,
  I have noticed myself getting intellectually lazier as I increasingly reach for them to replace my work instead of as a tool to make certain processes more
  efficient, or my work more productive. Therefore, I aim to use AI in this project only for things like coding, and not for the writing, the research, or the
  working-out of ideas.</p>

  <p>In practice, this web-log’s scope will be as broad as my interests are, and will take in general macroeconomics and market commentary, analysis of individual
  credits, tests of different models of credit analysis, evaluations of different investment strategies, and a closer look at the particular quirks and
  opportunities of different corners of the credit market.</p>

  <p>Finally, I want to note the creative debts this web-log owes. On typography and the general style of the site,
  <a href="https://www.legalstyle.co.uk/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Notes on the Style of Law</a> has been my main inspiration. As I write more,
  I will credit the people I have found most useful here.</p>

